WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.905
Look at this number: 348.
It is made of three digits.

2
00:00:09.125 --> 00:00:13.397
Each digit is worth what its
own column says. Let us look.

3
00:00:13.897 --> 00:00:18.355
Here is 348 in its columns:
hundreds, tens and ones.

4
00:00:18.575 --> 00:00:22.616
The 3 stands in the hundreds
column. It is worth three hundred.

5
00:00:22.836 --> 00:00:26.690
The 4 stands in the tens
column. Four tens is forty.

6
00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.718
The 8 stands in the ones column.
Eight ones is just eight.

7
00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:36.650
So 348 is not three, four, eight.
It is three hundred and forty-eight.

8
00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.448
Taking a number apart is
called decomposing it.

9
00:00:40.668 --> 00:00:44.243
348 comes apart into 300, and 40, and 8.

10
00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:48.968
Add the three parts back
together, and you have 348 again.

11
00:00:49.188 --> 00:00:53.600
Nothing is lost. Watch a
zero, though: 307 has no tens.

12
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.021
Its parts are just 300 and 7. The
zero holds the tens column open.

13
00:00:59.521 --> 00:01:02.215
There is more than one
way to break a number up.

14
00:01:02.435 --> 00:01:06.429
348 is 300 and 40 and 8.
Now move one hundred across.

15
00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:09.946
That one hundred crosses
over and becomes ten tens.

16
00:01:10.166 --> 00:01:16.528
Four tens and ten tens make fourteen
tens. So 348 is 200 and 140 and 8.

17
00:01:16.748 --> 00:01:20.138
The total has not changed
at all. That is regrouping.

18
00:01:20.638 --> 00:01:24.214
Multiplying by ten does
not add a zero on the end.

19
00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:27.499
It moves every digit
one column to the left.

20
00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:32.409
Take 35. The 3 is three
tens and the 5 is five ones.

21
00:01:32.629 --> 00:01:36.252
Times ten, and those three
tens become three hundreds.

22
00:01:36.472 --> 00:01:42.230
The five ones become five tens.
A zero holds the ones open. 350.

23
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.981
Rounding means picking
whichever neighbour is nearer.

24
00:01:46.201 --> 00:01:51.170
Round 348 to the nearest ten.
It sits between 340 and 350.

25
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:56.684
Halfway is 345. 348 is past
halfway, so it rounds up to 350.

26
00:01:56.904 --> 00:02:02.106
Now round it to the nearest hundred.
It sits between 300 and 400.

27
00:02:02.326 --> 00:02:08.131
Halfway is 350 now, so 348 goes down
to 300. Two answers, one number.

28
00:02:08.631 --> 00:02:12.067
Sometimes close is good
enough. That is an estimate.

29
00:02:12.287 --> 00:02:15.677
Do not try to count them
all. That takes far too long.

30
00:02:15.897 --> 00:02:19.752
Ring one small group instead.
There are about ten in it.

31
00:02:19.972 --> 00:02:23.548
Now see how many groups like
that fill the box. About twenty.

32
00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:29.248
Twenty groups of ten is two hundred.
There were 214, so that was close.

33
00:02:29.748 --> 00:02:35.878
A digit is worth what its column
says. 348 is 300 and 40 and 8.

34
00:02:36.098 --> 00:02:44.132
Regrouped, 348 is also 200 and 140
and 8. Times ten, 35 becomes 350.

35
00:02:44.352 --> 00:02:48.253
Round to the nearer neighbour,
and estimate in groups of ten.
